{"attack": true, "case": "The target claims we prefer highly corroborated theories because they are 'the most informative and precise tool available' and offer 'depth of insight,' while 'knowing [they are] likely false.' But 'informative about the world' and 'depth of insight' are themselves truth-conducive notions: a theory that is known to be false is not informative *about the world*—it is precisely misleading. If its content does not approximate truth, calling it 'informative' collapses into 'entertaining but wrong.' Thus the dissolution secretly depends on treating high informational content as a proxy for approximate truth, which is exactly the inductive bridge the claim purports to avoid. The target's own second countercondition names this fault yet the main claim provides no answer to it.", "counterexample": null, "premise": "If a theory is known to be likely false, its high 'informational content' cannot be informative about the world unless near-truth is valuable — an inductive assumption.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "bba47b2878603c883ce700a675ea2077909522c4eb3a60eedbef83fd8f225c99", "quote": "we prefer the highly corroborated theory because it is the most informative and precise tool available, despite knowing it is likely false."}, {"block": "bba47b2878603c883ce700a675ea2077909522c4eb3a60eedbef83fd8f225c99", "quote": "If 'informational content' is valued because it leads to truth, this valuing process may implicitly rely on inductive hopes."}], "successor_question": "Can 'informational content about the world' be defined in a way that is valuable even when the theory is known false, without any reliance on the theory approximating truth?"}